.Ql "tempel-map"
is a
.Ql "defvar-keymap" ,
not a defcustom, but is still user-configurable.
.Pp
\fB[?]\fP The suggested modern equivalent —
.Ql "cape-capf-trigger"
wrapped around
.Ql "tempel-complete"
— was \fInot\fP itself verified.
Treat as unverified.
.Ss 6.3 Template and file format [V]
.Ql ".eld"
files are a flat sequence
.Ql "(MODES PLIST TEMPLATES MODES PLIST TEMPLATES…)" .
Leading non-keyword symbols are mode names; keyword/value pairs form a per-section plist whose
.Ql ":when"
supplies a condition (defaulting to
.Ql "t" );
remaining conses are templates.
Mode matching is
.Ql "derived-mode-p"
plus
.Ql "major-mode-remap-alist"
indirection, with
.Ql "fundamental-mode"
sections always matching.
.Pp
Only
.Ql ":when"
is consumed from the \fIsection\fP plist — any other section-level keyword is \fBsilently discarded\fP.
Per-template
.Ql ":pre" Ns No "/" Ns Ql ":post"
are a separate mechanism: a trailing plist on each template cons.
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ql ":pre"
— a Lisp form evaluated \fBfirst\fP in
.Ql "tempel--insert" ,
before the element loop.
.It
.Ql ":post"
— stashed on the range overlay, evaluated by the finalizer
.Ql "tempel--done"
in the lexical scope of the named fields.
\fBNot evaluated on
.Ql "tempel-abort" Ns No ".\fP"
.El
.Pp
Element syntax (from
.Ql "tempel--element" Ns No "'s"
docstring): strings,
.Ql "nil" ,
.Ql "p" ,
.Ql "r" ,
.Ql "r>" ,
.Ql "n" ,
.Ql "n>" ,
.Ql ">" ,
.Ql "&" ,
.Ql "%" ,
.Ql "o" ,
.Ql "q" ,
.Ql "(s NAME)" ,
.Ql "(p PROMPT <NAME> <NOINSERT>)" ,
.Ql "(r PROMPT …)" ,
.Ql "(r> PROMPT …)" ,
.Ql "(l ELEMENTS…)" ,
and arbitrary Lisp forms dispatched first through
.Ql "tempel-user-elements" ,
with string results dynamically updated.
Upstream's own warning: \(dqUse caution with templates which execute arbitrary code!\(dq
.Pp
.Ql "tempel-auto-reload"
compares a whole
.Ql "(file . mtime)"
alist with
.Ql "equal" ,
so file \fBaddition and removal\fP also trigger a reload, not just mtime bumps.
.Pp
\fBBearing on this repo [I]:\fP
.Ql "templates/jotain.eld"
uses exactly the documented shape — bare mode symbols as section headers,
.Ql "p" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "n" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "n>" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "r>" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "q"
elements — and
.Ql "tempel-path"
is set to
.Ql "templates/*.eld" ,
which the wildcard support covers.
.Ss 6.4 hippie-expand — the prior art for a dwim chain key [V]
This is the most directly applicable finding for the \(dqseparate chain key\(dq design.
.Pp
\fBDefault
.Ql "hippie-expand-try-functions-list" Ns No "\fP"
(byte-identical on emacs-30, emacs-31 and master;
.Ql "hippie-exp.el" Ns No ":204-218):"
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&'(try-complete-file-name-partially
  try-complete-file-name
  try-expand-all-abbrevs
  try-expand-list
  try-expand-line
  try-expand-dabbrev
  try-expand-dabbrev-all-buffers
  try-expand-dabbrev-from-kill
  try-complete-lisp-symbol-partially
  try-complete-lisp-symbol)
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql ":type '(repeat function)" ,
autoloaded, no
.Ql ":version" Ns No "/" Ns Ql ":group" Ns No "/" Ns Ql ":set" .
Note the default is a \fBproper subset\fP of what the file defines —
.Ql "try-expand-line-all-buffers" ,
.Ql "try-expand-list-all-buffers"
and
.Ql "try-expand-whole-kill"
are deliberately excluded.
.Pp
\fBThe sanctioned way to build a bespoke chain key [V]\fP —
.Ql "make-hippie-expand-function" ,
autoloaded,
.Ql "(TRY-LIST &optional VERBOSE)" ,
returning an interactive closure that dynamically binds
.Ql "hippie-expand-try-functions-list"
to TRY-LIST and calls
.Ql "hippie-expand" :
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Pp
\(dqinstead of loading the variable with all kinds of try-functions above, it might be an idea to use
.Ql "make-hippie-expand-function' to construct different " Ns No "hippie-expand'-like"
functions, with different try-lists and \fBbound to different keys\fP.\(dq
.Pp
—
.Ql "hippie-exp.el"
Commentary, lines 114-118
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Ql "fset"
examples at lines 394-400.
Nothing in the file is marked obsolete
.No \&( Ns Ql "grep -n obsolete"
→ zero hits).
It is a \fBdefun returning a closure\fP, not a macro — the file's own preceding comment calls it \(dqa macro\(dq sloppily.
.Pp
\fBCycling semantics [V]:\fP repeated invocation steps through candidates and then forward through the try-list.
A positive numeric ARG jumps ARG functions forward; a negative arg,
.Ql "C-u 0" ,
or plain
.Ql "C-u"
\fBundoes\fP the tried expansion.
Three precisions: (a) it does \fBnot\fP wrap around — on exhaustion it messages \(dqNo further expansions found\(dq and
.Ql "(ding)" ;
(b) with no ARG a repeat re-invokes the \fIsame\fP try-function with
.Ql "old=t"
to get its next candidate, advancing only once that function returns nil — candidate-by-candidate, not one function per keypress; (c)
.Ql "C-u 0"
also reaches the undo branch, which the manual wording omits.
.Pp
\fB[R] Refuted 0-3:\fP the claim that the Commentary \(dqexplicitly sanctions\(dq the buffer-local/mode-hook pattern as \fIthe\fP documented pattern for prose-vs-code chains.
The text at lines 118-120 does offer that path — \(dqIt is also possible to make
.No "`hippie-expand-try-functions-list' a buffer local variable, and let it depend on the mode (by setting it in the mode-hooks)\(dq — but it carries no such normative weight."
.Ss 6.5 corfu's keymap — the RET and TAB question [V]
Default
.Ql "corfu-map" ,
from the corfu README:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
| Binding | Command |
| completion-at-point, TAB | corfu-complete |
| M-TAB | corfu-expand |
| RET | corfu-insert |
| next-line, down, M-n | corfu-next |
| previous-line, up, M-p | corfu-previous |
| C-g | corfu-quit |
| M-SPC | corfu-insert-separator |
| M-h | corfu-info-documentation |
| M-g | corfu-info-location |
.Ed
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Pp
\(dqThe current candidate is inserted with
.Ql "TAB"
and selected with
.Ql "RET" Ns No ".\(dq"
.Ed
.Pp
Upstream documents freeing RET explicitly:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
;; Free the RET key for less intrusive behavior.
;; Option 1: Unbind RET completely
(keymap-unset corfu-map "RET")
;; Option 2: Use RET only in shell modes
(keymap-set corfu-map "RET" `( menu-item "" nil :filter
                               ,(lambda (&optional _)
                                  (and (derived-mode-p 'eshell-mode 'comint-mode)
                                       #'corfu-send))))
.Ed
.Pp
This is decisive for the \(dqEnter must never accept a completion\(dq requirement: it is a \fBsupported, upstream-documented configuration\fP, not a hack.
The
.Ql "menu-item"
.Ql ":filter"
form is also the general pattern for making a corfu binding context-conditional.
.Pp
\fBNot established this round:\fP exhaustive accepted-value lists for
.Ql "corfu-preselect" ,
.Ql "corfu-on-exact-match" ,
and
.Ql "corfu-quit-no-match" .
The README shows
.Ql "corfu-preselect"
values
.Ql "prompt"
and
.Ql "directory"
by example only, and
.Ql "corfu-on-exact-match"
via
.Ql "'insert"
by example.
.Ql "corfu-quit-no-match"
is described with
.Ql "separator"
(default) and
.Ql "t" .
\fBDo not reproduce a complete value table from this report\fP — read the docstrings before relying on any value not listed here.
.Ss 6.6 What remains unresearched
Reported as \fBnot found\fP rather than inferred.
Two rounds produced zero verified claims on these, so the design document must not present them as evidence-backed:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
\fBCapf ordering, concretely (GAP 3).\fP Whether
.Ql "cape-capf-nonexclusive"
around
.Ql "eglot-completion-at-point"
is upstream-endorsed; how it compares with
.Ql "cape-capf-super"
merging; whether
.Ql "completion--capf-wrapper" Ns No "'s"
prefix-
.Ql "try-completion"
approximation for
.Ql ":exclusive 'no"
\fBmisfires under orderless\fP (the
.Ql "minibuffer.el"
FIXME says non-prefix completion \(dqwill not work (or not right) for completion functions that are non-exclusive\(dq — and this config runs orderless); and the behavior of
.Ql "eglot--capf-session"
/
.Ql "eglot--capf-session-flush" ,
including whether
.Ql "cape-capf-buster"
is needed around eglot.
.Pp
The two things GAP 3 \fIcan\fP lean on from verified findings: tempel's capfs are both
.Ql ":exclusive 'no" ,
and the ispell capf sits at depth 10 — so tempel and ispell both fall through, and neither shadows eglot.
.It
\fBOpt-out / kill-switch idiom (GAP 5).\fP Neither the normative half (Elisp manual Defining Variables / Customization / Minor Mode Conventions on
.Ql "defcustom"
vs
.Ql "defvar" ,
.Ql ":set" Ns No "/" Ns Ql ":initialize" ,
load-time vs runtime togglability) nor the descriptive half (Doom module flags, Prelude, Crafted, purcell, Prot, karthink, minimal-emacs.d) was gathered.
\fBThe design document's opt-out section is therefore grounded in this repo's own established convention\fP, which is verifiable locally, rather than in external evidence.
.It
\fBPerformance (GAP 6).\fP Nothing on
.Ql "corfu-auto-delay" Ns No "/" Ns Ql "-prefix"
tuning guidance, the per-keystroke cost of
.Ql "corfu-auto--post-command" ,
.Ql "eglot-send-changes-idle-time"
or capf latency reports,
.Ql "cape-dabbrev-check-other-buffers"
cost,
.Ql "corfu-history"
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
savehist boundedness, or native-comp's effect on completion responsiveness.
Every performance number in circulation for this stack should be treated as folklore until measured.
.El
.It
